# carino-offline — example Caddy sites. NOT ACTIVE.
#
# The packaged Fedora Caddyfile ends with `import Caddyfile.d/*.caddyfile`, so
# this file is inert purely because of its .example extension. Rename it to
# carino-offline.caddyfile to turn it on, then `systemctl reload caddy`.
#
# The extension is doing real work: caddy.service runs
# `caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile` as ExecStartPre, so a config
# naming hostnames this machine cannot answer for does not degrade the web
# server — it stops it from starting, on the box that also serves the mirror
# and the documentation you would use to fix it.
#
# Replace EXAMPLE.INTERNAL throughout.
#
# SELINUX — caddy is CONFINED here, which matters the moment a site needs to
# write. Fedora's caddy package labels /usr/bin/caddy httpd_exec_t in its
# %post (`semanage fcontext --add --type httpd_exec_t /usr/bin/caddy`), and
# policycoreutils-python-utils is present in this chain, so the relabel really
# does happen at build time and caddy transitions to httpd_t like Apache does
# — it is not an unconfined domain.
#
# Everything /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/carino-offline.conf creates under
# /var/lib/carino-offline gets the policy default var_lib_t, and httpd_t is
# allowed to READ var_lib_t and not to write it. Read-only serving — the
# mirror, www and the kiwix proxy below — therefore works as written.
# Anything that needs caddy to WRITE (uploads, an access log kept under
# /var/lib/carino-offline) will fail with an AVC and nothing else, so relabel
# that path first:
#
#   semanage fcontext -a -t httpd_sys_rw_content_t '/var/lib/carino-offline/<path>(/.*)?'
#   restorecon -R /var/lib/carino-offline/<path>
#   # and check with: ausearch -m avc -ts recent
#
# The reverse proxy needs no such step: the caddy package also sets
# `setsebool -P httpd_can_network_connect on`.
#
# Not yet verified on installed hardware — no ISO from this image has been
# booted, so the above is read from the shipped policy and the package's own
# scriptlet, not from a running enforcing system.

# --- The package mirror -------------------------------------------------
# Plain HTTP on purpose. dnf verifies RPM signatures against Fedora's own keys
# on the client, the machines that most need this repo are fresh installs that
# do not have the internal root yet, and a mirror unreachable until TLS works
# is a bootstrap deadlock on a network with no internet to break it.
http://mirror.EXAMPLE.INTERNAL {
	root * /var/lib/carino-offline/mirror
	file_server browse
}

# --- The fleet, served locally ------------------------------------------
# The Fleet Offline Edition unpacked into www. Every tool is client-side
# static with no CDN and vendored libraries, so this is a file server and
# nothing more — which is why the offline edition is packaging work rather
# than a rewrite.
#
# /whoami is the one route that is not a file. The fleet's shared "Sys.
# Status" panel (carino-diag.js) asks four public services what a visitor's
# address is; on a LAN with no route out, all four fail, and the panel on a
# network tool reports nothing about the network it is on. This answers the
# same question from the connection itself — no lookup, no third party, and
# it works with the WAN cable pulled, which is the whole premise of the site
# being served here. Point the panel at it:
#
#   <script src="carino-diag.js" data-whoami="/whoami" defer></script>
#
# It is tried before every public provider, and it hands back whichever
# family the browser reached this server over — the panel sorts v4 from v6
# itself and falls through for the one it did not get.
# Two `handle` blocks rather than a bare file_server plus one: `handle` is
# mutually exclusive and terminal, so this says in the config what the
# routing actually is instead of leaning on Caddy's directive order.
hub.EXAMPLE.INTERNAL {
	encode zstd gzip
	tls internal

	handle /whoami {
		respond "{http.request.remote.host}"
	}

	handle {
		root * /var/lib/carino-offline/www
		file_server
	}
}

# --- Kiwix ---------------------------------------------------------------
# kiwix-serve listens on 127.0.0.1:8080 (see kiwix-serve.service — its own
# default is port 80, which would collide with this server). Proxying it means
# one TLS terminator, one access log and one port open on the firewall.
wiki.EXAMPLE.INTERNAL {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
	tls internal
}

# --- ACME for the rest of the LAN ---------------------------------------
# Caddy embeds smallstep's CA; acme_server exposes it so other machines can
# point an ACME client here and renew forever with no internet. This is the
# nearest thing available from a Fedora RPM to step-ca, which is NOT packaged
# — see the risks list in research/manifest-offline.json for what it does not
# cover (anything that is not an HTTP site, e.g. a printer or a switch, still
# needs easy-rsa by hand).
#
# Caddy's local root is generated on first start, on THIS machine, under
# /var/lib/caddy. It is deliberately not in the image: a CA key baked into an
# image is the same key on every install. Publish its fingerprint on the
# printed IP card and add the root to client trust stores.
acme.EXAMPLE.INTERNAL {
	acme_server
	tls internal
}
